From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 00:50:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Fold the mHC streams in registers in project_streams

Half of project_streams' time went to the post fold: per stream, scalar
shared loads of the staged streams, a staged BF16 tile, a TMA store that
waited for the write, and a reload into the GEMM tile, with a barrier
between each. The collapse's sum of squares then took sixteen block-wide
reductions, one per token.

Each thread now owns one token's 16 contiguous columns. It loads x and
the four streams into registers with 16-byte accesses, folds the previous
output in (same expression and order, so the updated streams, the GEMM
partials and the collapse are unchanged bit for bit), stores the updated
stream directly, and writes the GEMM tile. Both sums of squares
accumulate in registers and reduce across the token's eight lanes, in a
fixed order that does not depend on the batch.

With fn cold in L2, as in serving, a sublayer entry takes 14.4 us at one
token (18.5 before), 17.4 at 16 (20.9) and 44.4 at 96 (67.3).
